     "type": "long",
     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "1) When Tricki reached the surgery, the veterinary surgeon made his diagnosis at once: the dog had no organic disease, and his sickness was the direct result of overfeeding and lack of exercise. 2) He therefore decided that no medicine and no operation were required; what Tricki needed was discipline and a return to natural ways. 3) For the first two days the patient was given no food at all, only plenty of water, so that his overloaded system could rest; Tricki lay on his rug, mournful and weak, whining at nothing, but the vet stood firm and did not yield to his pleading eyes. 4) On the following days the starving dog's eyes brightened; when the other dogs were fed, Tricki hovered near them, sniffed at their bowls and, tempted by the smell and sight of plain food, began to eat. 5) He was then allowed to join the pack; he rolled on the grass, chased his new companions and plunged into their games with growing delight. 6) Day by day his fat melted away; he became lithe, hard muscled and full of bounce, with bright eyes and a ready bark. 7) Meanwhile the eggs, wine and brandy sent by his mistress were quietly enjoyed by the vet and his partners, for the dog needed none of them. 8) Thus the cure was a triumph not of surgery but of common sense, and Tricki bounded back to his owner in perfect health."
