     "answer": "Mathilde and her husband have a strained relationship from the beginning of the story. Mathilde is dissatisfied with her husband's job as a clerk and their modest lifestyle, often complaining and making him feel inadequate. Her husband, however, is devoted to her and tries his best to make her happy, such as saving money for a new dress and suggesting she borrow jewelry. After the loss of the necklace, their dynamic shifts dramatically. They are forced to work together to repay the debt, with Mathilde doing hard labor for the first time in her life. This shared struggle creates a sense of unity between them, though it is born of necessity rather than affection. By the end of the story, their relationship has transformed from one of discontent and dissatisfaction to one of shared hardship and mutual endurance, though the ten years of struggle have taken a toll on both of them."
